emily: opened PR #4899 to refactor the parsing helpers in docs/examples and add a concrete example for arrays that can contain strings, objects, or nulls. link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/redwood-docs/pull/4899 :tada:

david: nice. quick q: did you leave the original schema verbatim or did you simplify it? CI was failing on the previous example because readers assumed items were always objects.

emily: i simplified the schema and added an explicit note. current schema snippet I added: 

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "events": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "oneOf": [
          { "type": "string" },
          { "type": "object", "required": ["type"], "properties": {"type": {"type":"string"}, "payload":{"type":"object"}} },
          { "type": "null" }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

and below it i included a short paragraph: "Note: model outputs may include nulls; defensive parsing is recommended."

ria: can you paste the parsing examples? want to reuse for the node SDK quickstart.

emily: sure 00here are the two snippets i added to docs/examples/parsers.md:

python:

```python
resp = client.generate(prompt)
obj = resp.json()
for ev in obj.get('events', []):
    if ev is None:
        handle_missing()
        continue
    if isinstance(ev, str):
        handle_text_event(ev)
    else:
        ev_type = ev.get('type')
        if ev_type == 'open':
            handle_open(ev.get('payload', {}))
        else:
            handle_unknown(ev)
```

node (typescript):

```ts
const obj = await client.generate({ prompt });
for (const ev of obj.events || []) {
  if (ev === null) { handleMissing(); continue }
  if (typeof ev === 'string') { handleTextEvent(ev); continue }
  const evType = (ev as any).type;
  if (evType === 'open') { handleOpen((ev as any).payload || {}); }
  else { handleUnknown(ev); }
}
```

omar: love the explicit null handling. small nit: in the node snippet you use `(ev as any)` which might scare copy/paste users. maybe show a typed interface in a follow-up.

emily: good call. i'll add a short typed variant in a follow-up.

david: can we add a tiny pytest that runs these against fixtures? that'll prevent regressions when examples move.

emily: added tests under docs/examples/tests/test_parsers.py in the PR. fixtures: `['ping', {'type':'open','payload':{'id':1}}, null]`. passes locally.

ria: i'll mirror the python test into our node example with jest snapshot to keep parity.

